# Field Note 0006

## First Inconsistency

There is a tendency to imagine that important discoveries announce themselves dramatically.

In my experience, they do not.

They begin as administrative problems.

This one did.

Several months ago I noticed a broken cross-reference while reviewing older notes.

Broken links are not unusual.

I reorganize the index frequently, and mistakes are inevitable.

The reference pointed toward a file that did not appear to exist.

I assumed I had either renamed it or deleted it during an earlier revision.

I searched the project.

Nothing.

I searched my backups.

Nothing.

I searched older exports.

Nothing.

The reference remained.

Eventually I removed it.

I remember doing so because I was mildly irritated by it.

There is something strangely satisfying about eliminating small inconsistencies.

The project felt cleaner afterwards.

I thought no more about it.

Approximately a week later, the reference had returned.

Not merely restored through version control.

Rewritten.

Placed exactly where I had deleted it.

My first assumption was embarrassingly simple.

I concluded that I had forgotten removing it.

That explanation survived less than an hour.

The project maintains revision history.

The deletion was recorded.

The reappearance was not.

At this point the most likely explanation became software error.

I exported the index.

Rebuilt it.

Compared every file manually.

The reference remained.

The destination did not.

That distinction became increasingly important.

The problem was not that I had discovered an unfamiliar file.

The problem was that something inside the structure continued behaving as though the file already existed.

I spent the following weeks trying to reproduce the behaviour.

I failed.

The reference appeared ordinary.

Its formatting matched every other entry.

Its location made sense.

Nothing about it suggested corruption.

Only one detail separated it from every other cross-reference in the project.

I could not account for its origin.

That sentence deserves careful reading.

There are many things inside this index that I do not yet understand.

There was, until this point, nothing inside it that I could not explain having written.

Those are very different statements.

I have no conclusion to offer.

Only a decision.

From this point onward I began treating unexplained additions differently from unexplained interpretations.

If I could not establish how something entered the index, I stopped asking what it meant.

The first question had become more important than the second.

Looking back, I think this was the moment the project changed.

Not because I discovered anything extraordinary.

Because, for the first time, I could no longer assume I was the only author of its structure.
